th a superconducting material okay and  what happens is for magnetic resonance  imaging you need a very high magnetic  field and those high magnetic fields you  generate them by running a current  through a conductor but if it's a  regular conductor because they dissipate  a lot of energy it's very costly and  also very difficult to generate such  high magnetic fields however  in superconductors because the  electrical currents and superconductors  do not  dissipate any energy you can easily you  know establish very high currents in  these coils and you can generate very  large magnetic fields so pretty much all  magnetic resonance imaging systems in  the world in all of the hospitals in the  world actually used superconductors and  that's you know sort of one  widespread application of  superconductors  we have also superconductors in other  technologies for example  probably plenty of you have heard about  levitating trains okay the mac left that  you know there's some in japan and in  china so these  they make use of the fact that when you  run currents through this again through  the superconductors they they spell you  know  they generate the magnetic field and  that also if conversely if you have  magnetic field somewhere it cannot  penetrate the superconductor and that is  to repulsion okay between objects so  the way a magnetic you know levitated  train works is because you have these  superconductors and you have magnetic  fields and they are repelling the  magnetic field that creates a repulsive  force that you know levitates the trains  and because there is no contact between  the train and the you know the rail  tracks you know then you have very  little dissipation and that allows  actually the trains to go very very fast  okay so the superconducting limited  trains are the fastest strains that we  have in the world so that's those are  just two examples maybe we'll we'll talk  a little bit a little bit more about  something that some of you may have  h