d  their properties including looking for  materials that might  be relevant to superconducting  technologies  so right now our approach to looking for  superconductors is unfortunately a bit  of trial and error the problem is that  we don't understand completely all of  the ingredients that lead to  superconductivity  actually we can kind of think of two  classes of superconductors we can think  of conventional superconductors and  unconventional superconductors and the  conventional ones we tend to understand  quite well  but the problem is that they have very  low superconducting temperatures so this  uh  idea of a transition temperature between  a normal state and a superconducting  state is something that characterizes a  superconductor and we would like that  temperature to be as high as possible in  order to  be able to use it in um in real world  applications  so that we don't have to cool down using  say liquid nitrogen or material to make  it superconduct so these conventional  superconductors they're generally not  good for this they superconduct at  temperatures  well below minus 200 degrees celsius  very low temperatures  but they're a second class of  superconductors called unconventional  superconductors and these are the ones  that are more technologically uh  relevant because they have temperatures  that are are approaching um or  surpassing the boiling point of liquid  nitrogen so we can cool them in  hospitals to use them as  the magnets inside of mris  but widespread applications would  require them to reach you know much  closer to room temperature  and for that we don't have great ways of  predicting which materials  could form these superconducting states  and so when it comes to materials  discovery  we just have to make  a lot of attempts and see what happens  and hope for some serendipitous  discovery so we really would like to  better understand the ingredients for  superconductivity so that we could  design new superconductors that can be